        <source media="(max-width: 700px)" srcset="/images/generated/usb-c-dock-desk-v2-800.webp" />
        <img src="/images/generated/usb-c-dock-desk-v2.webp" width="1536" height="1024" alt="A laptop and monitor on a wooden desk, connected through a USB-C dock, with a small external drive beside it" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
    </figure>
    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Look up the exact laptop and its USB-C port capability. Read the host's documented external-display capability first. A second display may depend on MST, USB4/Thunderbolt display tunneling, or DisplayLink, depending on the host and dock. If the port qualifies, swap in the dock's supplied cable, install DisplayLink or vendor drivers, update the dock's firmware, and set the OS to extend rather than mirror.</p></div>
