      <figcaption>A second monitor needs every link in the chain to support it. The laptop port (highlighted) is the most common limit, so start there.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Confirm the host port carries video at all.</h2><p>USB-C is a connector shape. The port behind it may carry USB data only, USB data plus DisplayPort Alt Mode, USB4, or Thunderbolt 3 or 4. A data-only USB-C port cannot carry native DisplayPort Alt Mode video. DisplayLink is a separate USB graphics path that can work over USB data with compatible hardware and supported drivers. Check OS support and whether managed-device policy permits the software before buying; it is not a promise of native-display performance. Find the laptop's official specification page and read the language for the specific port you are using.</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Record the exact laptop model and OS version.</li><li>Find the documented capability of the port you plug the dock into.</li><li>Reject the "one port must be the same as the other" assumption — many laptops label each USB-C port differently.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Check how many display streams the host actually allows.</h2><p>A video-capable USB-C port can expose different display capabilities depending on the host. A second monitor may come from MST, multiple display streams tunneled over USB4/Thunderbolt, or DisplayLink USB graphics. The exact model specification matters more than the connector label.</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>DP Alt Mode + MST</strong><p>MST can carry multiple DisplayPort streams through one link when the host and operating system support that use. Windows commonly supports extended displays through MST hubs; macOS generally does not use DisplayPort MST hubs for independent extended desktop displays.</p></div><div><strong>Thunderbolt or USB4</strong><p>USB4 and Thunderbolt can tunnel display traffic, but the number, resolution, and refresh rate of supported displays remain specific to the host GPU, port implementation, dock, and display link.</p></div><div><strong>DisplayLink</strong><p>USB graphics displays carried as data with DisplayLink software. Compatibility is broad, but latency, compression, DRM, and workload considerations make it a different tradeoff from native GPU display output.</p></div></div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Identify the dock's video mode, then match it to the host.</h2><p>Docks fall into the three categories above and sometimes mix them. Some Thunderbolt docks offer USB-C fallback modes, but capabilities in fallback mode are model-specific and can be substantially reduced. Check the dock's compatibility table rather than assuming all ports remain functional. A DisplayLink dock plugged into any host will do nothing extra until its driver is installed.</p><ol class="article-steps"><li><strong>Read the spec sheet, not the marketing.</strong><span>Look for "Thunderbolt 4," "USB4," "MST," or "DisplayLink" as literal words.</span></li><li><strong>Cross-reference with the host capability from Step 01.</strong><span>A DP-Alt-Mode-only host with a non-DisplayLink single-stream dock is a one-display setup.</span></li><li><strong>If the host is Apple silicon, read the chip name.</strong><span>Apple's native external-display limits vary by Mac model and generation. Check Apple's current specification for the exact Mac; do not infer the limit from 'base' versus 'Pro/Max' alone.</span></li></ol><div class="note">macOS generally does not support using a DisplayPort MST hub to extend one DisplayPort link into multiple independent desktops. Apple-silicon display limits also vary by model, so verify the exact Mac and dock rather than counting dock outputs.</div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Replace the host cable with a known full-featured one.</h2><p>The USB-C cable between the laptop and the dock is the most common single point of failure. A USB-C cable that only supports USB 2.0/charging can still power a dock and carry low-speed USB data, but it lacks the high-speed lane wiring used for native DisplayPort Alt Mode. Use the cable that shipped with the dock. If it is missing, buy a rated USB4 or Thunderbolt cable of the exact length you need — longer passive cables lose signal integrity.</p><p>The USB-C cable buying guide on this site covers what the wattage and speed ratings actually mean and how to identify an unlabeled cable.[1]</p></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 05</p><h2>Install DisplayLink or vendor drivers and update the firmware.</h2><p>DisplayLink docks require the free driver from Synaptics on both Windows and macOS. Native-display docks may work without a separate display driver, but host drivers, dock firmware, and vendor utilities can still matter. Check support for the exact dock and host before returning hardware.[2]</p><ul class="article-list"><li>Windows: install the vendor's dock utility, then check for a firmware update button.</li><li>macOS: follow the dock vendor's documented update procedure and required permissions for that exact utility.</li><li>Reboot after firmware update, then re-plug the dock with the laptop already on.</li></ul></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Step 06</p><h2>Set the OS to extend, not mirror.</h2><p>A dock can correctly present two displays that the operating system then silently mirrors. Confirm the arrangement before assuming the second display is missing.</p><div class="article-cards"><div><strong>Windows</strong><p>Settings &gt; System &gt; Display &gt; scroll to "Multiple displays" &gt; "Extend these displays." Then drag the display icons so their arrangement matches physical position.</p></div><div><strong>macOS</strong><p>System Settings &gt; Displays. Click the second display, uncheck "Mirror Displays" if set, then use the "Arrangement" tab to place it.</p></div></div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>When the diagnosis says the dock is fine.</h2><p>Most single-display failures resolve at Step 01, 04, or 05. If the host port simply cannot carry a second stream and you cannot change laptops, a DisplayLink dock is the honest fix. If you already own a Thunderbolt-capable host and want a documented dual-display path, the two docks below are the ones on this site with a full fit guide.</p><div class="cards">
        <a class="card" href="/gear/connectivity/caldigit-ts4"><h3>CalDigit TS4 fit guide</h3><p>18-port Thunderbolt 4 dock for a manufacturer-documented Thunderbolt-capable host and a high-demand desk.</p></a>
        <a class="card" href="/gear/connectivity/anker-553-usb-c-hub"><h3>Anker 553 USB-C hub</h3><p>A smaller connection set for a single external display and a few low-bandwidth peripherals.</p></a>
        <a class="card" href="/guides/usb-c-dock-compatibility"><h3>Before you buy: dock compatibility</h3><p>The buying-side companion to this troubleshooting guide — host port, displays, power, bandwidth, and cables.</p></a>
      </div></section>

      <section><p class="page-kicker">Related fixes</p><h2>Keep troubleshooting the same system.</h2><div class="cards"><a class="card" href="/guides/usb-c-charging-display-problems"><h3>USB-C charging and display hub</h3><p>Start with the full USB-C troubleshooting map.</p></a><a class="card" href="/guides/how-to-tell-if-usb-c-port-supports-video/"><h3>Does this USB-C port support video?</h3><p>Confirm the host display path before diagnosing the dock.</p></a><a class="card" href="/guides/usb-c-dock-compatibility"><h3>USB-C dock compatibility</h3><p>Verify host display and dock capabilities before replacing hardware.</p></a></div></section>
